<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01998</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f8fea744-60c8-4aa7-8588-83b2762eb23f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <exercise id="af85d497-4717-4fc4-b6c7-2aacde1d05e9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Each represents the height of an object being launched into the air as a function of time.</para>
            <para id="para-00003">Here are graphs of functions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">
                      <media alt="Graphs of functions f and g. Vertical axis, height. Horizontal axis, time. Function f begins low on the y axis, increases in height quickly, then ends at about halfway down the x axis. Function g begins high on the y axis, moves gradually downward, and ends toward the end of the x axis.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_16_11_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00005">Which object was launched from a higher point?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00006">Correct! The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because its <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is above the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00007">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is: The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because its <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is above the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4beb467c-0d2f-4c24-8881-b4da9aa1f763" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Which object reached a higher point?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Correct! The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because the vertex is higher.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">The correct answer is: The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because the vertex is higher.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c35c6425-b4dc-4a7b-aad6-ad79eadee4c0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Which object was launched with the greater upward velocity?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00014">Correct! The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because the “slope” near the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is greater. It increases in height much more quickly than the other object.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">The correct answer is: The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because the “slope” near the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is greater. It increases in height much more quickly than the other object.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a6dc30bc-7294-4722-b69f-d4256a3b6afc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00017">Which object landed last?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00018">Correct! The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because the positive <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of its graph is to the right of the positive <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00019">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00020">The correct answer is: The object represented by function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> because the positive <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of its graph is to the right of the positive <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1d3584d2-ddc0-45ef-9f9f-eeacd9c57fcf" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            
            <para id="para-00021">The function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> models the height of a ball in feet, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> seconds after it was thrown.</para>
            <para id="para-00022">Find one of the zeros of the functions using <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct! -0.5. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>0.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The zero of the function is the same value.</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is -0.5. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>0.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The zero of the function is the same value.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fd7745a3-fd5a-4fb2-a162-97e44cdd044d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            
            <para id="para-00026">Find one of the zeros of the functions using <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00027">Correct! 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The zero of the function is the same value.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">The correct answer is 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The zero of the function is the same value.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e1af55f5-cc64-4b14-9a0e-6834b1cdaa8b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00030">What do the zeros tell us in this situation?</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00032">The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">The negative zero represents the height, in feet, when the time is zero.</para>
            <para id="para-00034">The positive zero represents the time, in seconds, when the basketball reaches the maximum height.</para>
            <para id="para-00035">The negative zero represents the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00036">Correct! The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
            <para id="para-00037">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00038">The correct answer is: The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a699ac98-cd91-467f-9025-d5fef8e137fd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00039">Are both zeros meaningful?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00040">Correct! No. Only the positive zero is meaningful. It tells us the ball hits the ground after 1 second.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00042">Incorrect. The correct answer is No. Only the positive zero is meaningful. It tells us the ball hits the ground after 1 second.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_705d5199-4f67-4611-ae30-30ff51aa5a85" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            
            <para id="para-00043">From what height, in feet, is the ball thrown?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00044">Correct! 8 feet. When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is rewritten in standard form it is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The constant term (which corresponds to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept) tells us the height of the basketball when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or when it is launched.</para>
            <para id="para-00045">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00046">The correct answer is 8 feet. When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is rewritten in standard form it is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The constant term (which corresponds to the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept) tells us the height of the basketball when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or when it is launched.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_29628f71-310e-42f0-886f-689c67ea88a4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            
            <para id="para-00047">When, in seconds, does the ball reach its highest point?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00048">Correct! 0.25 seconds. Graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00049">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00050">The correct answer is 0.25 seconds. Graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5ab1f1c1-e6d5-43fa-9777-85412039fd00" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00011">
            
            <para id="para-00051">How high, in feet, does the ball go?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00011">
            <para id="para-00052">Correct! 9 feet. Graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1. Then evaluate the function at 0.25 to get a height of 9.</para>
            <para id="para-00053">Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.3 – 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00054">The correct answer is 9 feet. Graphing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1. Then evaluate the function at 0.25 to get a height of 9.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e3be73f2-3cb1-46b0-9b1d-e06668db6158" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00012">
            <para id="para-00055">The height in feet of a thrown football is modeled by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, where time <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is measured in seconds.</para>
            <para id="para-00056">Which term in the equation describes how many feet above the ground the football was thrown?</para>
            <para id="para-00057">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00058">The constant term 6</para>
            <para id="para-00059">The linear term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00060">The squared term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00061">The squared term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00012">
            <para id="para-00062">Correct! The football was thrown from 6 feet above the ground.</para>
            <para id="para-00063">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00064">The correct answer is the constant term 6. The football was thrown from 6 feet above the ground.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4db11e62-1a36-48c7-8734-ce008601fe62" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00013">
            <para id="para-00065">Which term in the equation describes the initial upward velocity of the football in feet per second?</para>
            <para id="para-00066">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00067">The constant term 6</para>
            <para id="para-00068">The linear term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00069">The squared term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00070">The squared term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00013">
            <para id="para-00071">Correct! The initial upward velocity of the football was 30 feet per second.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00073">The correct answer is the linear term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. The initial upward velocity of the football was 30 feet per second.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ca5820b3-7859-4636-b616-c1c22fe6dcfe" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00014">
            <para id="para-00074">How does the squared term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> affect the value of the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
            <para id="para-00075">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00076">The squared term decreases the value of the function.</para>
            <para id="para-00077">The squared term increases the value of the function.</para>
            <para id="para-00078">The squared term has no effect on the value of the function.</para>
            <para id="para-00079">The value of the function is zero when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> is zero.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00014">
            <para id="para-00080">Correct! The squared term decreases the value of the function because the values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> are being subtracted from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00081">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00082">The correct answer is: The squared term decreases the value of the function because the values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> are being subtracted from <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>30</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5d14125b-5d97-4607-9fa6-d7202ce485dc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00015">
            <para id="para-00083">What does this term <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> reveal about the situation?</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00085">The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.</para>
            <para id="para-00086">The initial upward velocity of the football.</para>
            <para id="para-00087">How far the football was thrown above the ground.</para>
            <para id="para-00088">How long the ball is in the air before it hits the ground.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00015">
            <para id="para-00089">Correct! The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.</para>
            <para id="para-00090">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00091">The correct answer is: The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_321a83e9-c3ef-4fff-a1bc-23a581947f8e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00016">
            
            <para id="para-00092">The height in feet of an arrow is modeled by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is seconds after the arrow is shot.</para>
            <para id="para-00093">At what time, in seconds, does the arrow hit the ground?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00016">
            <para id="para-00094">Correct! The positive zero is 2.25 because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is equal to zero when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.25</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The zeros mean the times, in seconds, when the arrow is on the ground. Only the positive zero makes sense. The negative zero would represent a time before the arrow was launched.</para>
            <para id="para-00095">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00096">The correct answer is 2.25. The positive zero is 2.25 because <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is equal to zero when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.25</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The zeros mean the times, in seconds, when the arrow is on the ground. Only the positive zero makes sense. The negative zero would represent a time before the arrow was launched.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_96622d8a-7db3-4b49-921b-b8debb4eee4a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00017">
            
            <para id="para-00097">At what height, in feet, is the arrow shot?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00017">
            <para id="para-00098">Correct! 18 feet. When the arrow is shot, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 0 and h(0)=(1+2⋅0)(18−8⋅0)=(1)(18)=18.</para>
            <para id="para-00099">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para id="para-00100">The correct answer is 18 feet. When the arrow is shot, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 0 and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a9419388-3cfa-45be-9186-903475b2c12e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00018">
            <para id="para-00101">5. Two objects are launched into the air.</para>
            <para id="para-00102">The height, in feet, of Object A is given by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>32</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:msup><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00103">The height, in feet, of Object B is given by the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:msup><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00104">In both functions, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is seconds after launch.</para>
            <para id="para-00105">Which object was launched from a greater height?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00018">
            <para id="para-00106">Correct! Object A. The objects are launched when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>32</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00107">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00108">The correct answer is: Object A. The objects are launched when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>32</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>40</m:mn><m:mi>⋅</m:mi><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_214ffa65-1827-44d9-bc43-c1b25a0f12fa" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00019">
            <para id="para-00109">Which object was launched with a greater upward velocity?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00019">
            <para id="para-00110">Correct! Object B.The coefficient of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gives the upward velocity and 40 is greater than 32.</para>
            <para id="para-00111">Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00112">The correct answer is: Object B. The coefficient of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gives the upward velocity and 40 is greater than 32.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content></document>
